\begin{inshort}
If a lead-cooled fast reactor ever leaks, iodine from the coolant can travel
through hot gas and stick to cooler surfaces. Laboratory experiments blow such
vapours through a tube whose wall runs from hot to cold and then measure
\emph{where} they land. We wrote a computer model that predicts the same
thing. Instead of building it in one go, we built it as a \textbf{ladder of
24 small cases}: each one copies the previous case, changes exactly
\emph{one} thing, and comes with the numbers that prove the change works.
The top rungs compare the model with real experiments: for five published
\PbI{} experiments it predicts the temperature where deposits start to
within 18\,K on average (RMS), half the error of the two models of the
original paper, with nothing tuned to the data.
\end{inshort}

