\section{The question}

Lead--bismuth eutectic (LBE) is the liquid-metal coolant of some fast reactor
designs. Fission products dissolved in it, iodine among them, can evaporate,
and iodine does not leave as pure iodine: it combines with lead and bismuth
into gases such as \PbI{} and \BiI{}. Which compound forms matters, because
each one condenses at a different temperature, and therefore ends up in a
different place.

To study this, radiochemists use \textbf{thermosublimatography}: a long,
narrow tube (a few millimetres wide, about a metre long) whose wall
temperature falls steadily from about 1000\,K at one end to near room
temperature at the other. A small boat of material is heated near the hot
end; a slow stream of helium carries the vapour downstream; each compound
sticks to the wall where the wall is cold enough for it. Afterwards the tube
is scanned, and the position of each deposit tells you its
\textbf{deposition temperature}, \Tdep{}.

The physics in the tube is simple to state and awkward to compute: the gas
\emph{flows} along the tube, the vapour \emph{diffuses} sideways to the wall,
and at the wall it \emph{condenses} or \emph{re-evaporates} depending on the
local wall temperature and on what is already deposited there. The goal of
the LESTO work in Task 3.4 is a computational fluid dynamics (CFD) model,
built on OpenFOAM, that does all three and can be checked against the
experiments.

\begin{jargon}
\textbf{Jargon in plain words.}\enspace
\textbf{Carrier}: the helium gas that flows through the tube and carries
the vapour. \textbf{Frozen carrier}: we compute the helium flow once and
then keep it fixed; the trace vapour is far too dilute to change it.
\textbf{Mass fraction $Y$}: how much of the gas, by mass, is a given
species. \textbf{Wall law}: the rule that says how fast vapour leaves the
gas and sticks to the wall (or comes back). \textbf{HKS}: the
Hertz--Knudsen--Schrage law, the textbook evaporation/condensation rate,
driven by the difference between the actual vapour pressure and the
equilibrium vapour pressure $p_\mathrm{eq}(T)$ of the solid or liquid.
\textbf{Ledger / closure}: the bookkeeping of every kilogram (released, in
the gas, on the wall, out of the tube); the \emph{closure} is what is left
unexplained, ideally zero. \textbf{Plug flow}: a simple hand-calculation
of the same tube, used as a sanity check. \textbf{GEMS3K}: a
chemical-equilibrium code that can tell the model which gases coexist.
\end{jargon}

