\section{Climbing the ladder}

\subsection{Rungs 001--007: get the helium flowing \hfill\normalfont\small\color{PSIgray}Bojan Ničeno}
The first case is the plainest possible flow: helium through a pipe of
2.4\,mm radius and 0.69\,m length, at room temperature, on a mesh of
228,960 cells. Each next case adds one realistic touch: a parabolic
velocity profile at the inlet (002) and inside the pipe from the start
(003, which cuts the solver iterations from 388 to 222), the energy
equation (004), a wall temperature that falls along the tube (005), a
density that follows the ideal-gas law (006), and finally temperature-dependent
viscosity and conductivity with a wall falling from 1000\,K to 300\,K
(007, Fig.~\ref{fig:early}a). The saved result of 007 is the helium
``carrier'' that the next rungs read.

\subsection{Rungs 008--011: move vapour through it \hfill\normalfont\small\color{PSIgray}Bojan Ničeno}
Now the flow is frozen and a new solver, \texttt{rhoFixedFlowFoam}, moves
trace species through it. Case 008 sends \PbI{} vapour and an inert tracer
with identical settings and checks that they stay identical (they do, to
the last digit). 009 gives \PbI{} its real, temperature-dependent
diffusivity, up to 25 times the tracer's on the hot side. 010 introduces a
\emph{solid} species, the deposit, and the interface through which
chemistry will later talk to the flow. 011 couples them: what leaves the
gas appears as solid on the wall, and the two sources add up to exactly
zero (Fig.~\ref{fig:early}b).

