\subsection{Rungs 012--021: teach the vapour how to stick \hfill\normalfont\small\color{PSIgray}Jan Kren}
Up to 011 the deposition rule was a placeholder. From 012 on, the cases add
real wall physics, again one piece at a time:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \case{012} The simple \emph{temperature model} of Lobresco et
    al.~\cite{Lobresco2026}: vapour sticks once the wall is colder than a
    set \Tdep{}. It also introduces a vapour source (a ``boat'') and the
    \textbf{mass ledger}: every step, every kilogram is accounted for. The
    unexplained remainder stays at about $10^{-16}$ of the released amount,
    i.e.\ at the limit of computer arithmetic (Fig.~\ref{fig:wall}a).
  \item \case{013} The physical wall law (HKS): condensation and
    re-evaporation driven by the equilibrium vapour pressure of solid and
    liquid \PbI{}, and a boat that evaporates its real inventory.
  \item \case{014} A cheap synthetic test channel (1000\,K to 350\,K)
    that runs in 10\,s. Here the model's \Tdep{} can be checked against the
    plug-flow hand calculation: 561.2\,K against 567.1\,K, within the
    10\,K gate (Fig.~\ref{fig:wall}b).
  \item \case{016} The vapour has to \emph{diffuse} to the wall before it can
    condense; this extra resistance turns out to control the uptake. It is
    used in every realistic run after this one.
  \item \case{017} The real helium flow and measured wall temperature of
    one Lobresco experiment, instead of the synthetic channel.
  \item \case{018--021} More chemistry: a second iodide (\BiI{}), nine
    Pb--Bi--I gases that re-balance with each other everywhere in the tube
    (019; 020 does the same with GEMS3K), and metal vapour from LBE with
    shared deposits and wall reactions (021). In 021, all five deposit onsets
    land within 3.9\,K of the hand calculation.
\end{itemize}

