\section{The top rungs: does it match reality?}

The last three cases do not add physics; they \emph{test} it.

\textbf{022 --- against another code.} Lobresco et al.\ computed their
experiments with the code T-Flows using the simple temperature model. In
``paper mode'' our model repeats their calculation: the gas level agrees
with their Fig.~9 to 0.5\,\%, and all ten lines of their Table~2 agree within
the resolution of the wall temperature table. The one visible difference,
a deposit starting 2.6\,cm earlier, disappears (to 0.9\,mm) with the
T-Flows parameter set; which set made the published figures is a question
for the authors.

\textbf{023 --- against five experiments.} The five \PbI{} experiments of
Lobresco et al.~\cite{Lobresco2026} differ in helium flow (5, 106 and
540\,mL/min) and duration (6 to 300\,s). We ran all five with the HKS wall
law, the measured wall temperatures, and \emph{no parameter adjusted to the
data}, then applied the paper's own rule for \Tdep{} (the point where the
deposit reaches 1\,\% of its peak). Table~\ref{tab:023} and
Fig.~\ref{fig:bench}a show the outcome: an RMS error of 18\,K, against
35\,K for both models of the original paper. A coarser mesh changes the
answers by at most 0.3\,K, and the mass ledger closes to $3.3\times10^{-16}$.

